\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {19.0.1}{\ignorespaces The sum segment tree over the running array \(A\) (bottom row: the leaves, labelled by index \(1..16\)). Every node \(t_i\) owns the interval under it and stores the \emph  {sum} of that interval; the green subscripts show the eight size-2 nodes' intervals, and each larger interval is the union of its children's. Fold-up checks reused everywhere: \(t_8{=}A_1{+}A_2{=}5\), \(t_4{=}t_8{+}t_9{=}5{+}9{=}14\), \(t_2{=}t_4{+}t_5{=}14{+}10{=}24\) (the sum of slots \(1..8\), CSES~1648's first query), and \(t_1{=}t_2{+}t_3{=}24{+}25{=}49=\DOTSB \sum@ \slimits@ A\). Swap the fold for \(\min \) or \(\max \) and the very same picture gives the tables below. The leaves \(t_{16..31}\) equal \(A\), so this array cross-checks with Ch.18's~Figure~\ref {fig:c14-array} at the leaf layer (the internal nodes differ: interval aggregates, not lowbit blocks).}}{1147}{figure.caption.201}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:c13-tree}{{19.0.1}{1147}{The sum segment tree over the running array \(A\) (bottom row: the leaves, labelled by index \(1..16\)). Every node \(t_i\) owns the interval under it and stores the \emph {sum} of that interval; the green subscripts show the eight size-2 nodes' intervals, and each larger interval is the union of its children's. Fold-up checks reused everywhere: \(t_8{=}A_1{+}A_2{=}5\), \(t_4{=}t_8{+}t_9{=}5{+}9{=}14\), \(t_2{=}t_4{+}t_5{=}14{+}10{=}24\) (the sum of slots \(1..8\), CSES~1648's first query), and \(t_1{=}t_2{+}t_3{=}24{+}25{=}49=\sum A\). Swap the fold for \(\min \) or \(\max \) and the very same picture gives the tables below. The leaves \(t_{16..31}\) equal \(A\), so this array cross-checks with Ch.18's~\figref {c14-array} at the leaf layer (the internal nodes differ: interval aggregates, not lowbit blocks)}{figure.caption.201}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {19.0.2}{\ignorespaces The chapter's six-state taxonomy, read top to bottom as an escalation. Rows 1--2 are the machine (intervals, the three recursions); row~3 is a change of \emph  {what a node stores} (a tuple, not a scalar); row~4 runs the tree \emph  {downward} (search instead of fold); row~5 is a change of \emph  {layout} (iterative \(2n\), no recursion); row~6 is a change of \emph  {boundary} -- knowing when this is a lazy (Ch.20), persistent (Ch.21), or decomposition (Ch.22) problem after all. Every solved problem sits on exactly one row: CSES~1649 on rows~1--2, the CSES~1190 monoid on row~3, CF~1179C on row~4, CF~514D on row~5, and the Summit spanning rows~2 and~4 (fold + descend).}}{1148}{figure.caption.202}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:c13-taxonomy}{{19.0.2}{1148}{The chapter's six-state taxonomy, read top to bottom as an escalation. Rows 1--2 are the machine (intervals, the three recursions); row~3 is a change of \emph {what a node stores} (a tuple, not a scalar); row~4 runs the tree \emph {downward} (search instead of fold); row~5 is a change of \emph {layout} (iterative \(2n\), no recursion); row~6 is a change of \emph {boundary} -- knowing when this is a lazy (Ch.20), persistent (Ch.21), or decomposition (Ch.22) problem after all. Every solved problem sits on exactly one row: CSES~1649 on rows~1--2, the CSES~1190 monoid on row~3, CF~1179C on row~4, CF~514D on row~5, and the Summit spanning rows~2 and~4 (fold + descend)}{figure.caption.202}{}}
